  "content": {
    "id": "p64-q37-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "37",
    "prompt": "By first simplifying the expression, differentiate $f(x)=\\dfrac{x^4-1}{x-1}$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$f'(x)=3x^2+2x+1$, for $x\\ne1$.",
      "worked": "For $x\\ne1$,\n$$\\begin{aligned}\nf(x)&=\\frac{x^4-1}{x-1}\\\\\nf(x)&=\\frac{(x^2-1)(x^2+1)}{x-1}\\\\\nf(x)&=\\frac{(x-1)(x+1)(x^2+1)}{x-1}\\\\\nf(x)&=(x+1)(x^2+1)\\\\\nf(x)&=x^3+x^2+x+1\\\\\nf'(x)&=3x^2+2x+1.\n\\end{aligned}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 42
  },
